WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.214
Learner and Context Analysis.

2
00:00:03.214 --> 00:00:07.979
ID 553 - Module 3: Examining the Situation.

3
00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:14.627
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.227 --> 00:00:28.047
Every design decision assumes something about the learners:
what they already know, what they can attend to, what
motivates them, what tools they have, and how much time
they'll give you.

5
00:00:28.047 --> 00:00:33.806
If those assumptions are wrong, the instruction fails no
matter how well it's built.

6
00:00:33.806 --> 00:00:37.371
Learner analysis replaces assumptions with evidence.

7
00:00:37.371 --> 00:00:43.266
Context analysis does the same for the settings where
learning and performance happen.

8
00:00:43.266 --> 00:00:50.876
Skipping this step is the most common cause of instruction
that is accurate, beautifully produced, and useless.

9
00:00:51.476 --> 00:00:53.088
Here's why it matters.

10
00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:56.825
Two designers analyze the same four hundred nurses.

11
00:00:56.825 --> 00:01:05.399
The first works from assumption: nurses are busy adults who
dislike computers, so keep the training short and simple.

12
00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:27.676
The second collects data: a quarter of the nurses have used
the vendor's system at a previous employer; the night shift
will not attend daytime classes; sixty-one percent of last
time's help desk calls concerned five workflows; veteran
nurses report anxiety and younger nurses report boredom with
basics.

13
00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:31.340
The first designer builds one course for everyone.

14
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:38.521
The second builds a test-out path, shift-specific support,
and practice focused on five workflows.

15
00:01:38.521 --> 00:01:41.892
Same population, completely different designs.

16
00:01:42.492 --> 00:01:44.429
What should you find out?

17
00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:54.265
Entry skills and prior knowledge, including misconceptions,
because prior knowledge is the strongest predictor of new
learning.

18
00:01:54.265 --> 00:02:03.715
Attitudes toward the content, toward the delivery mode, and
toward the organization, because attitudes explain
resistance.

19
00:02:03.715 --> 00:02:09.214
Motivation: why are they here, and what happens if they
learn or don't?

20
00:02:09.214 --> 00:02:15.411
Educational and ability levels, including reading, language,
and digital skills.

21
00:02:15.411 --> 00:02:17.115
Group characteristics.

22
00:02:17.115 --> 00:02:24.938
And for adults, competing demands on time, experience you
can leverage, and the autonomy they expect.

23
00:02:24.938 --> 00:02:35.626
One caution: learning preferences can inform engagement, but
the idea of matching instruction to learning styles has no
empirical support.

24
00:02:35.626 --> 00:02:37.098
Don't design on it.

25
00:02:37.698 --> 00:02:39.221
How do you find out?

26
00:02:39.221 --> 00:02:43.409
Use more than one method, because each has blind spots.

27
00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:54.678
Interview a sample of learners and their supervisors, and
ask about the task rather than about training: walk me
through the last time you did this.

28
00:02:54.678 --> 00:03:03.739
Observe learners performing the task, and watch for
workarounds; they reveal what the current process fails to
support.

29
00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:07.546
Survey for breadth, keeping it short and specific.

30
00:03:07.546 --> 00:03:13.638
And mine existing data: performance records, help desk logs,
assessment results.

31
00:03:13.638 --> 00:03:19.120
Existing data is usually the most objective evidence and the
least used.

32
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.034
Triangulate.

33
00:03:20.034 --> 00:03:30.084
If interviews say learners are confident and the error logs
say they fail, believe the logs and ask where the confidence
comes from.

34
00:03:30.684 --> 00:03:33.600
Dick and Carey distinguish two contexts.

35
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:47.525
The performance context is where learners will use what they
learn: the physical setting, the social setting, the
managerial support, and whether the new performance is
expected and rewarded.

36
00:03:47.525 --> 00:03:53.867
If the performance context doesn't support the new behavior,
training won't produce it.

37
00:03:53.867 --> 00:04:01.813
The learning context is where instruction happens:
facilities, equipment, bandwidth, the LMS, and scheduling.

38
00:04:01.813 --> 00:04:05.677
The closer the two contexts, the better the transfer.

39
00:04:05.677 --> 00:04:12.092
That's why simulations, practice environments, and on-the-
job components matter so much.

40
00:04:12.692 --> 00:04:16.367
Analysis is only useful if it changes the design.

41
00:04:16.367 --> 00:04:19.367
For every finding, write an implication.

42
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:26.043
A quarter of nurses have used the system before, so offer a
test-out or accelerated path.

43
00:04:26.043 --> 00:04:34.518
Night shift won't attend daytime classes, so provide
asynchronous options and on-unit support during night hours.

44
00:04:34.518 --> 00:04:42.694
Help desk calls concentrated in five workflows, so
prioritize those for practice and build job aids for them.

45
00:04:42.694 --> 00:04:50.494
Two devices per three nurses on the unit, so design practice
that fits short sessions at shared devices.

46
00:04:50.494 --> 00:04:58.219
Your implications table is the deliverable of assignment
five, and the rest of the course builds on it.

47
00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:13.951
Avoid the common mistakes: describing learners in
demographics only, assuming everyone is the same, relying on
self-report, and analyzing where the training happens while
ignoring where the performance happens.

48
00:05:13.951 --> 00:05:27.785
For assignment five, use at least two methods, reach real
learners even if the sample is small, address every
category, analyze both contexts, and produce at least eight
specific implications.

49
00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:32.468
If you cannot reach your learners at all, reconsider the
project.

50
00:05:32.468 --> 00:05:37.873
A design built on imagined learners will not survive the
later assignments.
